      "published_at": "2020-12-31T15:01:44Z",
      "source": "arXiv API cached in E2",
      "source_file": "E2/data/raw_papers.jsonl",
      "source_file_sha256": "98036e1d99139a7fa4938362ab45f97fc12f21a91182c0ce5d22ee5be7d0c662",
      "source_locator": "E2/data/raw_papers.jsonl#line=18;byte_offset=32834",
      "source_retrieved_at": "2026-10-22T17:10:10.179092+00:00",
      "source_sha256": "c2a59191afa66c9c438cc4729bd3029fccb0f657c7e8713d3001e622552f8df1",
      "source_updated_at": "2020-12-31T15:01:44Z",
      "source_url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2012.15641v1",
      "text": "Title: Investigating Memorability of Dynamic Media\n\nAbstract: The Predicting Media Memorability task in MediaEval'20 has some challenging aspects compared to previous years. In this paper we identify the high-dynamic content in videos and dataset of limited size as the core challenges for the task, we propose directions to overcome some of these challenges and we present our initial result in these directions.",
      "title": "Investigating Memorability of Dynamic Media"
    },
    {
      "abstract": "Memorability determines what evanesces into emptiness, and what worms its way into the deepest furrows of our minds. It is the key to curating more meaningful media content as we wade through daily digital torrents. The Predicting Media Memorability task in MediaEval 2020 aims to address the question of media memorability by setting the task of automatically predicting video memorability. Our approach is a multimodal deep learning-based late fusion that combines visual, semantic, and auditory features. We used audio gestalt to estimate the influence of the audio modality on overall video memorability, and accordingly inform which combination of features would best predict a given video's memorability scores.",
      "abstract_sha256": "7d0e8e5a2ca7d2195812cdc372058b9a6c0e11845d06921b32b78d9c1ebba02a",
      "available_at": "2020-12-31T14:50:42Z",
      "paper_id": "2012.15635v1",
      "published_at": "2020-12-31T14:50:42Z",
      "source": "arXiv API cached in E2",
      "source_file": "E2/data/raw_papers.jsonl",
      "source_file_sha256": "98036e1d99139a7fa4938362ab45f97fc12f21a91182c0ce5d22ee5be7d0c662",
      "source_locator": "E2/data/raw_papers.jsonl#line=20;byte_offset=35576",
      "source_retrieved_at": "2026-10-22T17:10:10.179092+00:00",
      "source_sha256": "ee391f9a7cdc0d23a922a12a1d960c34ab740466e17522e6300f402f278868aa",
      "source_updated_at": "2020-12-31T14:50:42Z",
      "source_url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2012.15635v1",
      "text": "Title: Leveraging Audio Gestalt to Predict Media Memorability\n\nAbstract: Memorability determines what evanesces into emptiness, and what worms its way into the deepest furrows of our minds. It is the key to curating more meaningful media content as we wade through daily digital torrents. The Predicting Media Memorability task in MediaEval 2020 aims to address the question of media memorability by setting the task of automatically predicting video memorability. Our approach is a multimodal deep learning-based late fusion that combines visual, semantic, and auditory features. We used audio gestalt to estimate the influence of the audio modality on overall video memorability, and accordingly inform which combination of features would best predict a given video's memorability scores.",
      "title": "Leveraging Audio Gestalt to Predict Media Memorability"
    }
  ],
